UN Special Rapporteur on extrajudicial  summary or arbitrary executions has just  ended a two-week visit to Nigeria  Agnes calamar has been looking at  alleged abuses committed by security  forces in the north and south where a  separatist movement has been gaining  ground well calm art is warning the  Nigerian government to address what  she's calling a pressure cooker of  violence that is claiming thousands of  life a report also highlights excessive  use of force by police leading to  killings and lack of investigations and  prosecutions for those committing human  rights abuses with calamari says she  found an absence of accountability and a  lack of justice for victims  well Agnes Kalmar joins us live now from  Abuja and Agnes thank you very much for  joining us on the program that is a  pretty long list there tell us more  about what your a visit to Nigeria has  uncovered I noticed the phrase in your  press conference either the earlier a  pressure cooker of violence so what did  you find well I could not really use any  other words than pressure cooker I have  found localized and countrywide patterns  of violence that are simmering and my my  main concerns with those localized and  countrywide systems of violence is that  they are now taking place in a context  characterized by population explosion by  an increased number of people living in  extreme poverty by the proliferation of  weapons coming from throughout the  region of West Africa and by the  environmental degradation  so those external pressure applied on  top of those ecosystems eco political  systems of violence are ready to  conflate to explode  in a way which I don't think we have yet  completely understood so my my  preliminary finding today was really to  issue this this warning bus to the  government and to the international  community there is in Nigeria pressure  cooker that if it is unchecked could  have ripple effect throughout the  regions and now is the time to  prioritize the rule of law and  accountability for the people of Nigeria  Agnes you use the word crisis and said  in your press conference that you know  you don't want the government to pretend  this is not a crisis but how do they  view the situation that you bring up in  your report when you will have to ask  them I think I don't think I have made  any sharp or stark revelations to the  government whether or not authorities  are willing to admit the extent of the  problem is a different matter but in all  my meetings with officials in the  majority of my meetings with official  there is an acknowledgement of the scale  and the extent of the problems that  gives me some hope that the authorities  with the necessary leadership and  political will will be able to begin a  roadmap towards addressing the lack of  accountability towards ensuring that  killings particularly by the police but  not only that all the killings are  investigated that the systemic factors  such as the lack of resources of the  police a lack of training that all those  resources all those resources issues are  addressed let me remind you that  Nigeria is a middle-income country so I  have approached the questions of  arbitrary killings  within that context and what I have  found is not in keeping with what I  would expect from a middle-level country  a middle-income country so Agnes  calamari is what you're saying that  there are resources that can be put to  work in solving some of these problems  but the right priorities are perhaps not  being made yes I believe that at the  moment the rule of law and  accountability have not been  sufficiently prioritized I can take for  proof the fact that in 2006 one of my  predecessors went on a mission and when  I compared what he has found with what I  have found there are so little  differences between the two report it is  shocking there are 15 years apart so now  is the time to prioritize the rule of  law and accountability there is a  securitisation strategy to address many  problems but security without  accountability is not a solution for the  long term I'm not even sure it's a  solution for the short term it's just a  containment policy a containment in 2019  when the country is confronting the  global processes I have identified of  population explosion increase poverty  environmental degradation that  containment policy is not gonna work is  not gonna work so it is time more than  time too much time has been wasted it is  time to prioritize the rule of law and  accountability for the people of Nigeria  particularly for the 49 millions that  are living in extreme poverty did you  find that there was any positive change  yes yes I have found a few positive  changes now for instance in the  Northeast you you were mentioning the  the conflict against Boko Haram in  2015-2016  that region was at the center of many  concerns for human rights violations by  Boko Haram of course but also by the  security agencies there was a vast  number of allegations of arbitrary  killing disproportionate indiscriminate  bombing disappearances kill death in  custody massive deaths in custody in  fact now those problems have not been  investigated and there is still not any  accountability for those past violations  but the progress residing the fact that  allegations over the last two years 2018  and 2019 have decreased so for instance  allegations of arbitrary depravation of  life they are far less of them than they  were three years ago that I take as a  positive sign now I would like this  positive sign to be to those positive  sign I would like to see steps taken to  ensure accountability for past crimes I  would particularly like the many  disappearances that have been brought to  my attention by the children the wives  the daughters and and and son of many  disappeared men I would like for those  disappearances to be addressed as a  matter of urgency because the torture it  is creating for the people left behind  cannot be under underestimated agonistic  Halima they're joining us UN Special  Rapporteur thank you very much for  joining us thank you
 

